s it  presented itself with a contamination  that too today is still being tolerated  by both the regulatory community as well  as the research community let's advance  to the next slide and see what our  options are  the options to solve this problem were  actually done some 35 years ago in a  publication  in the journal virology  in which we engineered a very specific  plasmid that as you can see in the  cartoon  in the middle  that at the very bottom of the  illustration of the itr it was designed  to carry a unique restriction site  this re unique restriction site was pbu2  which did not cut in the aav genome and  if you advance you'll see  that  what we have on the left hand side if  you advance again it's a circle  illustrating where the unique  restriction sites were  and they surgically removed the plasmid  backbone away from the aav packaging  sequences  that's illustrated on this there's a  restriction site a claw one that's a  unique cutter so if you advance what  you'll see is that we took three  different substrates  the pvu2 surgical removed plasmid  backbone  the linear av vector or the circular aav  vector and we introduce all of those  into an aav replication system and if  you advance one more time you'll see the  results of this where it illustrates  very graphically  that the av genome that was cleaved away  from the plasmid sequence  actually replicated more efficiently and  generated large amounts of viral genomes  suggesting that  if one could artificially remove this  resolution step one could generate more  material in the end and basically a  cleaner material  if you go to the next slide you'll also  see on  that slide label back to the future  we were able to show that in this  specific plasmid there was a phage  polymerase origin of replication and  then if you infected these cells with  the phage  you would be able to amplify this  plasmid backbone and that if you  purified it in this case it's being  purified as a single-stranded genome and  digest it with the pv2 enzyme you could  again separate the aav sequences away  from the bacterial plasma  this however was complicated by the fact  that it was all done in the e coli and  generated single-stranded genomes  and it wasn't until on present day  technology that we were able to revisit  this concept and actually utilize it as  shown in the next slide  on the left hand side you see once again  the wrapping cap plasmid illustrated  below  you also see that  when you resolve the wrapping cap  plasmid about 4 000 nucleotides are  represented by the rep and cap gene and  about 3 000 come from the plasmid  backbone  if you advance  you'll see that today  there has been an innovation in the  technology where one can add a  telomerase sequence at the end of the  aav itrs  and as a recognition of the sequence by  a phase polymerase one's able to again  surgically remove the plasmid backbone  from the aav synchronouses of interest  and you ask yourself how do we do this  how it's shown in the next slide  and if you look  c6 nucleotide recognition sequence this  sequence is recognized by if you advance  the phi 29 polymerase and it starts to  amplify on a rolling circle replication  mechanism  a very high fidelity uh amplification  and if you go advance again you'll see  that the protolimerase will then  recognize this cleavage sequence and  we'll cut and seal the ends  as a consequence of that you now have  linear dna that's closed at either end  and then if you advance you can see well  by a cleaning up technology one can  remove the plasmid sequences by dna's  degradation  and as a result if you go to the next  slide you see there's a very strong  product being generated as illustrated  on an argoros gel  this product is the aav molecule with  closed ends and completely absent of any  of the bacterial sequences  now the power of this is that all of  it's done in a test tube and there's no  bioreactors or contamination with a  plasmid at any level  if you 