hin days of two weeks  we also see that the av titers are  equivalent it's not superior to the  plasmid approach and that transduction  at least at these early stages seems to  be generating a much more potent reagent  further studies are being done to  evaluate this  enhanced transduction whether or not the  contamination of plasmid dna interferes  with vectors  uh suggesting a lower transduction  efficiency  and when you look for the denovo genome  there's  no  none in the plasma and then there is in  the doggy bones so what we're hoping to  say  in this  tutorial is that a  new technology has advanced out of the  community supporting research that was  done years ago but allowing one to  synthesize dna in the test tube to  generate all of the starting material  that's required to produce aav vectors  which are seeing  a lot of success in the clinic as well  as  new approaches for treating many  diseases from neurological to muscle  to bleeding disorders and i'll go to my  last slide and stop and acknowledge that  aspire is a wholly owned subsidiary of  bear  which has a  production component called virogen  that's in san sebastian spain that  generates all the aav vectors that are  used in our clinical trials it's also a  manufacturing facility for anyone who  wants to generate aav vectors to go into  the clinic  the doggy bone technology originated  from touch light touch touchlight is a  company in england that developed the  polymerase  manufacturing in e coli and we thank our  colleagues at gene whiz who we  collaborate on a regular basis  thank you and i'll stop there take any  questions  and thank you dr samolsky for that  great presentation we will now start the  q a portion of the webinar and if you  have any questions you want to ask  please do so now just put them into the  ask a question box and click send so  let's get started it looks like dr  samuelski we already have some great  questions coming in  are there any challenges you have  encountered wh